u know kazakhstan whatever estonia  we gotta look after those people because  that's our nation that's this whole  nationalism thing which was dormant for  so long is now becoming more powerful  so and then you have in the ukraine  you have  the nationalism of the ukrainian  speaking largely ukrainian-speaking  western central ukrainians  where  the language becomes a sign of their  national identity and then you have the  people in the east and south  of the ukraine  some of whom are russians who moved  there under the soviet union when they  were building up the industrial base  there  some of whom uh i mean personally  there's probably so much intermarriage  and so many of them simply speak russian  that uh even people with ukrainian names  who speak russian culturally russian i  don't see how you can distinguish those  people from  someone who for two or three generations  has lived in in ukraine speaks russian  and whose family originally came from  russia and i gather there's a lot of  family connections across the border  cultural connections and so forth so if  it turns out and i don't know this but  if it turns out that those in the  western ukraine have a sense of their  nationalism which is built around  different history being under  poland the polish lithuanian whatever  kingdom being under the austro-hungarian  empire being under poland  uh therefore more oriented towards the  west  and if the those in the east were under  the czars or under the soviet union  uh are culturally connected to the  russian world  if russian nationalism is rising  then how attractive is that to those  russian speakers or russian ethnic  people cultural russians who live in the  east and south probably quite attractive  um so and if i look at the history of  ukraine it seems that every five years  either the eastern and southern  ukrainians vote in their party  of  people who  corrupt leaders who basically skim large  amounts of money out of the system and  then every the other uh period they get  people from the west to do the same  equally disorganized equally um  you know ineffective and equally corrupt  so uh it seems as if there's a constant  see-sawing between the east and the west  so what i don't know is how strong is  the feeling of of ukrainian-ness  in the  in the east and south and of course  these kinds of feelings of identity can  change  uh again having studied czech  uh german speakers  were  30 of the population of czechoslovakia  between the wars  and i gather for an initial 15 years or  so  they were quite happy to participate  in czech or czechoslovakian political  life complaining that they didn't get  enough language rights complaining about  x y and z but still participating in the  government but then as german  nationalism grew  and with a lot of propaganda beamed at  them from  hitler's germany more and more of these  columns to dating  wanted to be part of germany now to be  fair  at the end of the first world war  the german speakers in the  austro-hungarian empire that is the  sudan germans the austrians  the people in in what's known as alto  adige  tyrol  in what is now italy but they're german  speakers they all wanted to be part of  the same country and they weren't  allowed to do that  because the allies wanted to weaken  germany and so they lived separately  so all of a sudden germany is on the  rise  and stirs up these people and now these  people are very unhappy and they have to  be part of germany and so germany uses  all of this as justification for what  led up to the munich agreement  and they're also german speakers in  poland so germany now this  nationalist germany  feels they have a right and an  obligation to look after their brothers  and sisters who obviously are being  maltreated  in these other countries and so this now  is a similar scenario to what we're  seeing in ukraine you have on the one  hand the western ukrainians who had a  great time there they overthrew  um yanukovych  but 