ean there's a  reason why  we had these demonstrations in in  ukraine uh yanukovych created conditions  where a lot of people were unhappy and  my understanding is  a lot of people demonstrating in the  maidan the freedom demonstrators were  not necessarily  all committed to joining the european  union a lot of it had to do with their  dissatisfaction with the government  corrupt it had been increasingly  restricting  uh you know the press and the media it  put  this uh political opponent timoshenko in  jail  uh so the i mean to the extent that  yanukovych was in power he's responsible  for the events of the maidan they  happened under his watch  and i think the news will come out that  a lot of the brutality and the sniper  activity and so forth were also directed  by yanukovych so the fact that someone  was elected doesn't mean that  that and if that elected person is  continuing to  to abuse  their position  then there may very well be a popular  uprising  subsequent to that  the uh a majority of the members of the  uh of the uh verhovna radar the the  parliament in fact  uh agreed to work together to form this  new government  a new government which in my opinion  should have been some kind of a  coalition government and i think that  was initially proposed and i think that  the  the western ukrainians who dominate this  new government are far too strident in  insisting on their agenda you know the  first thing they tried to do was change  the language laws which they have to you  know  give away on  uh and you know in in their comments  about russia they're so  strident  um you know i think  torchino the president said that uh  crimea will become russia's afghanistan  and putin's afghanistan and stuff like  this there's just a lot of  unnecessary aggravation there but  fundamentally to me  it's an issue of of the clash of  nationalisms and the big question to me  is  where is the allegiance of the people in  the eastern and southern ukraine  and the unfortunate thing is that  because those people are basically  plugged into the  russian media they are being totally  bombarded with  the russian  uh you know propaganda message  and i think the government in kiev  should be thinking about how they can  reach out to and persuade those people  that they'll be better off in ukraine  rather than  being stridently you know anti-putin  anti-russia and all the rest of it  they've kind of somehow and i think in  that sense i read again in the ukrainian  because i'm trying to read ukrainian in  the ukrainian newspaper online that  they're they've taken sort of uh  photo displays of the maidan to various  cities in eastern ukraine and they're  trying to explain to them  why they think that changes that that  that they're hoping to bring about  will improve  living conditions uh throughout ukraine  because after all i am sure that the  ukrainians everywhere would like to  live better earn more money not have  politicians that that build that siphon  off millions and billions of dollars and  build  extravagant mansions and and so forth  they would like to be governed better  and they would like to live  probably  even though the allegiance in the east  may be more towards a russian eurasian  community they would like to live  under the same conditions and  institutions as exist in western europe  without necessarily having to ally  themselves uh with western europe so  my take on all of this is that we're  talking about a clash of nationalisms  nationalisms that were kind of kept  under the lid  during the soviet period and and by the  way it's not just ukraine if you go to  latvia i haven't been to estonia but in  latvia  the latvians now have this sense that  their  little group of one and a half million  people uh is threatened  because there's 40 russians there  can those russian speakers buy into the  latvian national agenda or are they a  perennial sort of  outpost of the russian diaspora a  potential fifth column  that's a big co