 thing is that  because those people are basically  plugged into the  russian media they are being totally  bombarded with  the russian  uh you know propaganda message  and i think the government in kiev  should be thinking about how they can  reach out to and persuade those people  that they'll be better off in ukraine  rather than  being stridently you know anti-putin  anti-russia and all the rest of it  they've kind of somehow and i think in  that sense i read again in the ukrainian  because i'm trying to read ukrainian in  the ukrainian newspaper online that  they're they've taken sort of uh  photo displays of the maidan to various  cities in eastern ukraine and they're  trying to explain to them  why they think that changes that that  that they're hoping to bring about  will improve  living conditions uh throughout ukraine  because after all i am sure that the  ukrainians everywhere would like to  live better earn more money not have  politicians that that build that siphon  off millions and billions of dollars and  build  extravagant mansions and and so forth  they would like to be governed better  and they would like to live  probably  even though the allegiance in the east  may be more towards a russian eurasian  community they would like to live  under the same conditions and  institutions as exist in western europe  without necessarily having to ally  themselves uh with western europe so  my take on all of this is that we're  talking about a clash of nationalisms  nationalisms that were kind of kept  under the lid  during the soviet period and and by the  way it's not just ukraine if you go to  latvia i haven't been to estonia but in  latvia  the latvians now have this sense that  their  little group of one and a half million  people uh is threatened  because there's 40 russians there  can those russian speakers buy into the  latvian national agenda or are they a  perennial sort of  outpost of the russian diaspora a  potential fifth column  that's a big concern especially with a  strident russian nationalism which is  bound to infect  russians living over the border in  latvia and so these are all very  interesting questions of how much  you know the latvians would like to  convert all those russians into latvians  uh is that going to happen i don't know  so those are some of the issues um i see  these as a canadian you know as very  interesting because  my attitude here in canada where we  receive a lot of immigrants is  um  i would like those people to assimilate  i would like them and that has typically  been the case in the past that whether  they come from the ukraine and the  ukrainians  initially were the big promoters of  multiculturalism and getting government  funding for ukrainian language programs  and you know money for them to paint  easter eggs and dance in their national  costumes and stuff which i think they  should do  on their own ticket and it's not  something that i think the canadian  government should promote uh we  shouldn't prevent them obviously they do  what they want and i don't even think we  should force we shouldn't force  assimilate people but we should  encourage people to feel that  whoever their neighbor is whether  they're from china afghanistan portugal  you know japan doesn't matter they're  canadian so that there is a sense of  solidarity and you're building up a  sense of identity and identity is a  subjective thing and it moves and  changes depending on a lot of  circumstances and i think that's also  going to be an issue in the ukraine  is is where is the identity of those  people in in eastern ukraine and quite  frankly i don't know i don't know what  it is but hopefully  that  these kinds of issues can be resolved in  a peaceful way  rather than  the kind of  precedent that we have  uh in the 1930s so there's my little  ramble just to get started and i look  forward to opinions and ideas and hope  to do more of these bye for now
 

